### TL;DR  
`queryRenderedFeatures()` returns **plain JavaScript objects**.  
If a property’s value is itself an object you read it exactly the way you would read any nested object in JavaScript:

```js
const features = map.queryRenderedFeatures({ /* … */ });

features.forEach(f => {
  // `myNested` is an object, not a primitive
  const nestedObj = f.properties.myNested;          // or f.properties['myNested']
  console.log(nestedObj);                           // → {a: 1, b: "foo"}

  // Access a field inside the nested object
  const a = nestedObj.a;                            // 1
  const b = nestedObj.b;                            // "foo"

  // If you need a JSON string:
  const json = JSON.stringify(nestedObj);
});
```

That’s all there is to it—no special Mapbox API call is required.

---

## Why does this work?

`map.queryRenderedFeatures()` simply returns a **deep‑cloned copy** of the GeoJSON feature(s) that are currently rendered on the map.  
The `properties` member of a GeoJSON feature is just a plain JavaScript object, and the GeoJSON spec allows any JSON value (string, number, boolean, `null`, **object**, or **array**) as a property value.  

So when your source data looks like:

```json
{
  "type": "Feature",
  "geometry": { "type": "Point", "coordinates": [0, 0] },
  "properties": {
    "id": 123,
    "myNested": {
      "a": 1,
      "b": "foo",
      "c": { "deep": true }
    }
  }
}
```

the feature you get back from `queryRenderedFeatures()` will have exactly the same structure, and you can treat `myNested` as any other JavaScript object.

---

## When you *can’t* use the nested value directly

Mapbox GL JS styling and filter expressions **only understand primitive values** (string, number, boolean, null).  
If you want to use a nested value in a style expression or a filter, you have two options:

| Option | How to do it | Pros | Cons |
|--------|--------------|------|------|
| **Flatten the property in the source data** | Add a top‑level property that contains the value you need, e.g. `"myNested_a": 1` | Works with any style/filter expression; no extra runtime work | Requires preprocessing of your source data |
| **Derive the value at runtime** | In your JavaScript code, read the nested object, compute the needed value, and then set a *new* property on the feature (or on a separate data structure) that you use for styling/filtering | No need to touch the source file | You have to keep the derived property in sync if the source changes |

Example of flattening during data preparation:

```js
// Suppose `rawGeojson` is the original data you load into a GeoJSON source
rawGeojson.features.forEach(f => {
  const nested = f.properties.myNested;
  if (nested) {
    f.properties.myNested_a = nested.a;   // now you can filter on "myNested_a"
    f.properties.myNested_b = nested.b;
  }
});
```

After that you can write a filter like:

```js
map.queryRenderedFeatures({
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'myNested_a'], 1]
});
```

---

## Quick checklist

| ✅ | What to remember |
|---|-------------------|
| ✅ **Access** – `feature.properties.myNested` (or `['myNested']`). |
| ✅ **Drill down** – `feature.properties.myNested.innerKey`. |
| ✅ **Stringify** – `JSON.stringify(feature.properties.myNested)` if you need a string. |
| ✅ **Styling / filtering** – flatten the value first; Mapbox expressions can’t read inside an object. |
| ✅ **Mutability** – The returned feature is a *clone*; mutating it does **not** affect the source data. |

---

## Full example (including a filter that uses a flattened field)

```js
// 1️⃣ Load a GeoJSON source that contains a nested property
map.addSource('places', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/places.geojson'   // each feature has `properties.myNested`
});

// 2️⃣ When the source loads, flatten the nested value we care about
map.on('sourcedata', e => {
  if (e.sourceId !== 'places' || !e.isSourceLoaded) return;

  const source = map.getSource('places');
  const data = source._data; // internal reference to the GeoJSON object

  data.features.forEach(f => {
    const nested = f.properties.myNested;
    if (nested && typeof nested === 'object') {
      // Example: we want to style by the `a` field inside the nested object
      f.properties.myNested_a = nested.a;
    }
  });

  // Re‑set the source so the map picks up the new flat property
  source.setData(data);
});

// 3️⃣ Now we can style / filter using the flat property
map.addLayer({
  id: 'places-circle',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'places',
  paint: {
    'circle-color': [
      'case',
      ['==', ['get', 'myNested_a'], 1], '#f00',
      '#00f'
    ]
  }
});

// 4️⃣ Query rendered features and read the original nested object
map.on('click', 'places-circle', e => {
  const features = map.queryRenderedFeatures(e.point);
  features.forEach(f => {
    const nested = f.properties.myNested; // original nested object
    console.log('Nested object:', nested);
    console.log('a value (also available flat):', f.properties.myNested_a);
  });
});
```

---

### Bottom line
* **Reading** a nested property from `queryRenderedFeatures()` is just normal JavaScript object access.  
* **Using** that nested value in Mapbox GL style or filter expressions requires you to flatten the value (or compute a derived property) because the expression language only works with primitives.  

If you only need the data for your own JavaScript logic, you’re good to go with the simple `feature.properties.myNested.xxx` syntax. Happy mapping! 🚀

