Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe for “on‑the‑fly” icons in **MapLibre GL JS** when the style’s sprite does not contain the image you need.  
It works for a single icon, for many icons that you discover at runtime, and even for data‑driven icon selection.

---

## 1. The core idea – `map.addImage()`

MapLibre can render any image that you **add to the map’s image registry** at runtime:

```js
map.addImage(name, image, options?)
```

* `name` – the string you will later reference in `icon-image`.
* `image` – an `HTMLImageElement`, `HTMLCanvasElement`, `ImageBitmap`, or raw `Uint8Array`/`ArrayBuffer`.
* `options` – e.g. `{sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2}` (SDF = signed‑distance‑field, useful for recoloring).

If the image is not already in the style’s sprite, you simply load it yourself (via `fetch`, `new Image()`, or `map.loadImage`) and register it with `addImage`. Once registered, any layer that uses `icon-image: 'my‑icon'` will instantly start drawing the new picture.

---

## 2. Minimal working example (single icon)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – dynamic icon demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Wait until the style is fully loaded before we start adding images/layers
  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Load the image (you can also use map.loadImage for cross‑origin URLs)
    const img = new Image();
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';          // important for remote URLs
    img.src = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/marker.png';

    img.onload = () => {
      // 2️⃣ Register it under a name of your choosing
      map.addImage('my-dynamic-icon', img, { sdf: false });

      // 3️⃣ Add a layer that uses the new image
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'dynamic-point',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [{
              type: 'Feature',
              geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.447303, 37.753574] },
              properties: {}
            }]
          }
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'my-dynamic-icon',
          'icon-size': 1.0,
          'icon-allow-overlap': true
        }
      });
    };

    img.onerror = (e) => console.error('Failed to load icon', e);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What’s happening?**

1. The map loads its style (which does *not* contain `my-dynamic-icon`).
2. We create an `Image` object, set `crossOrigin = 'anonymous'` (required for most CDN‑hosted PNGs), and wait for `onload`.
3. `map.addImage('my-dynamic-icon', img)` registers the image.
4. The newly added layer references the image by name, and the icon appears instantly—no page reload needed.

---

## 3. Loading many icons on the fly (e.g. from a server)

If you have a list of icon URLs that you discover at runtime (maybe each feature has its own logo), you can loop over them, load each one, and add it to the map **once**. After that you can use a data‑driven `icon-image` property.

```js
// Example: an array of objects {id: 'restaurant', url: 'https://…/restaurant.png'}
const iconCatalog = [
  { id: 'restaurant', url: 'https://example.com/icons/restaurant.png' },
  { id: 'cafe',       url: 'https://example.com/icons/cafe.png' },
  { id: 'park',       url: 'https://example.com/icons/park.png' }
];

// Helper that returns a Promise that resolves when the image is ready
function loadAndAddImage(name, url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => {
      try {
        map.addImage(name, img);
        resolve(name);
      } catch (e) {
        reject(e);
      }
    };
    img.onerror = reject;
    img.src = url;
  });
}

// Call after map has emitted 'load'
map.on('load', async () => {
  // Load all icons in parallel
  await Promise.all(iconCatalog.map(icon => loadAndAddImage(icon.id, icon.url)));

  // Now add a source that references those icons via a feature property
  map.addSource('places', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: {
      type: 'FeatureCollection',
      features: [
        // each feature carries the name of the icon it wants to use
        { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.4, 37.75] }, properties: { icon: 'restaurant' } },
        { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.41, 37.76] }, properties: { icon: 'cafe' } },
        { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.74] }, properties: { icon: 'park' } }
      ]
    }
  });

  // Symbol layer that reads the icon name from the `icon` property
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'places-symbol',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'places',
    layout: {
      'icon-image': ['get', 'icon'],   // data‑driven: use the feature's `icon` property
      'icon-size': 0.8,
      'icon-allow-overlap': true
    }
  });
});
```

**Key points**

* **One‑time registration** – `addImage` must be called *once* per name. Subsequent calls with the same name will throw an error unless you first `map.removeImage(name)`.
* **Data‑driven `icon-image`** – the expression `['get', 'icon']` tells MapLibre to look up the image name from each feature’s property.
* **Parallel loading** – `Promise.all` speeds things up, but you can also load lazily (see section 4).

---

## 4. Lazy‑load icons *only when they are needed*

Sometimes you have **hundreds** of possible icons, and you don’t want to download them all up front. You can intercept the “missing‑image” error, fetch the image, add it, and then **re‑draw** the layer.

```js
// 1️⃣ Listen for style errors that indicate a missing image
map.on('error', (e) => {
  const msg = e?.error?.message ?? '';
  const missingMatch = msg.match(/does not exist in the style sprite: (.+)$/);
  if (!missingMatch) return; // not an image‑missing error

  const missingName = missingMatch[1];
  console.warn(`Icon "${missingName}" missing – loading it now…`);

  // 2️⃣ Build the URL for the missing icon (your own convention)
  const url = `https://example.com/icons/${missingName}.png`;

  // 3️⃣ Load and add it, then force a repaint
  loadAndAddImage(missingName, url)
    .then(() => {
      // The layer will automatically pick up the new image on the next render.
      // If you need an immediate refresh, you can call:
      map.triggerRepaint();
    })
    .catch(err => console.error('Failed to load dynamic icon', err));
});
```

**Why this works**

* When a layer tries to draw an icon that isn’t in the sprite *or* the runtime image registry, MapLibre throws an error that bubbles up as a `map.on('error')` event.
* By catching that error, you can fetch the missing asset, register it, and the next render cycle will succeed without any further code changes.

---

## 5. Using **SDF** (Signed‑Distance‑Field) icons for runtime recoloring

If you want to change the color of an icon on the fly (e.g., a generic pin that can be tinted per‑feature), load the image as an SDF and then use the `icon-color` paint property.

```js
// Load an SDF PNG (usually a black‑and‑white silhouette)
loadAndAddImage('pin-sdf', 'https://example.com/icons/pin-sdf.png')
  .then(() => {
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sdf-pins',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'places',
      layout: {
        'icon-image': 'pin-sdf',
        'icon-size': 1.2,
        'icon-allow-overlap': true
      },
      paint: {
        // Pull the color from a feature property called `color`
        'icon-color': ['get', 'color']
      }
    });
  });
```

*Make sure the PNG is prepared as an SDF (the alpha channel encodes distance). Most icon‑design tools can export this format, or you can generate it with Mapbox’s `sdf` CLI.*

---

## 6. Updating an existing image (e.g., animated icons)

If you need to **replace** an image that’s already registered (perhaps you fetched a higher‑resolution version after the first render), use `map.updateImage(name, newImage)`:

```js
// After the map is already showing 'my-dynamic-icon' at low resolution:
loadAndAddImage('my-dynamic-icon-hires', highResUrl).then(() => {
  const hiResImg = map.getImage('my-dynamic-icon-hires');
  map.updateImage('my-dynamic-icon', hiResImg);
  // Optionally remove the temporary entry
  map.removeImage('my-dynamic-icon-hires');
});
```

`updateImage` swaps the bitmap in‑place, so the layer does **not** need to be removed or re‑added.

---

