Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe for “on‑the‑fly” icons in **MapLibre GL JS** when the style’s sprite does not contain the image you need.  
It works for a single icon, for many icons that you discover at runtime, and even for data‑driven icon selection.

---

## 1. Why the error happens

When a layer’s `layout.icon-image` (or `layout.text-field` with an icon) refers to a name that is **not** present in the style’s sprite, MapLibre throws an error like:

```
Error: Image "my‑dynamic‑icon" does not exist in the style sprite.
```

The fix is to **add the image to the map’s image registry** *before* the layer tries to use it.  
MapLibre provides two ways to do that:

| API | When to use | What you give it |
|-----|-------------|------------------|
| `map.loadImage(url, callback)` | You have a URL (or data‑URI) to an image file (PNG, SVG, JPEG, …) | The raw `HTMLImageElement` (or `ImageBitmap`) |
| `map.addImage(name, image, options?)` | You already have an `HTMLImageElement`, `ImageBitmap`, `Canvas`, or `ImageData` (e.g. generated on the client) | The image object directly |

Both APIs end up calling `map.addImage`, which registers the image under the supplied **name** (the same string you would use in `icon-image`).

---

## 2. Minimal “one‑off” example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – dynamic icon demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Wait until the style is fully loaded
  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Load the image from a URL (could be a data‑URI, CDN, etc.)
    const iconUrl = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/marker.png';

    map.loadImage(iconUrl, (err, image) => {
      if (err) {
        console.error('Failed to load image', err);
        return;
      }

      // 2️⃣ Register it under a name that the layer will reference
      const iconName = 'my-dynamic-icon';
      if (!map.hasImage(iconName)) {
        map.addImage(iconName, image);
      }

      // 3️⃣ Add a source + layer that uses the new icon
      map.addSource('my-points', {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: {
          type: 'FeatureCollection',
          features: [{
            type: 'Feature',
            geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.447303, 37.753574] },
            properties: {}
          }]
        }
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-points-layer',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'my-points',
        layout: {
          'icon-image': iconName,   // <-- matches the name we added
          'icon-size': 1.2,
          'icon-allow-overlap': true
        }
      });
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What’s happening?**

1. `map.on('load')` guarantees the style’s sprite is already parsed.  
2. `map.loadImage()` fetches the PNG, decodes it, and gives you an `HTMLImageElement`.  
3. `map.addImage('my-dynamic-icon', image)` registers the image.  
4. The `symbol` layer’s `icon-image` points to that name, so the point renders correctly.

---

## 3. Adding many icons on the fly (e.g. per‑feature)

If you have a dataset where each feature may need a *different* icon that you only discover at runtime (e.g. icons stored in a database, user‑uploaded SVGs, etc.), you can:

1. **Pre‑process the data** to collect the distinct icon URLs (or raw data).  
2. **Load each icon once** and register it under a deterministic name (e.g. `icon-${hash}` or `icon-${id}`).  
3. **Store the name in a feature property** and use a data‑driven `icon-image` expression.

### Code pattern

```js
// Assume `features` is an array of GeoJSON features, each with a property `iconUrl`
async function registerIcons(features) {
  const nameByUrl = new Map(); // avoid duplicate loads

  for (const f of features) {
    const url = f.properties.iconUrl;
    if (!url) continue;               // no custom icon for this feature

    // Use the URL itself as a deterministic name (or hash it if you prefer)
    const name = `icon-${btoa(url).replace(/=/g, '')}`;

    // If we already added it, just store the name on the feature and skip loading
    if (map.hasImage(name)) {
      f.properties._iconName = name;
      continue;
    }

    // Load & add the image (wrap loadImage in a Promise for async/await)
    const image = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => err ? reject(err) : resolve(img));
    });

    map.addImage(name, image);
    f.properties._iconName = name; // store the name for later styling
  }
}

// Example usage:
map.on('load', async () => {
  const geojson = await fetch('https://example.com/points-with-icons.geojson')
                     .then(r => r.json());

  await registerIcons(geojson.features);

  map.addSource('dynamic-points', { type: 'geojson', data: geojson });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'dynamic-points-layer',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'dynamic-points',
    layout: {
      // Use the property we stored during registration
      'icon-image': ['get', '_iconName'],
      'icon-size': 0.8,
      'icon-allow-overlap': true
    }
  });
});
```

**Why this works**

- `icon-image` can be an **expression**. `['get', '_iconName']` reads the per‑feature property that holds the image name we added.
- The `registerIcons` function guarantees that each distinct URL is loaded **once**, then reused for every feature that references it.

---

## 4. Handling “missing‑icon” errors automatically

If you have a layer that already exists in the style and you want to **gracefully fall back** when an icon is missing, you can listen for the `error` event, detect the specific sprite‑lookup error, load the image, and then **re‑paint** the layer.

```js
map.on('error', e => {
  // MapLibre prefixes its own errors with "Error: ..."
  const msg = e?.error?.message ?? '';
  const missingMatch = msg.match(/Image "([^"]+)" does not exist in the style sprite/);
  if (!missingMatch) return; // not an icon‑missing error

  const missingName = missingMatch[1];
  console.warn(`Icon "${missingName}" missing – trying to load it dynamically.`);

  // Decide where to fetch the image from.
  // Here we assume a convention: icons are hosted at /icons/<name>.png
  const url = `/icons/${missingName}.png`;

  map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to load dynamic icon ${missingName} from ${url}`, err);
      return;
    }
    map.addImage(missingName, img);
    // Force the map to re-evaluate the style (idle will fire automatically)
    map.triggerRepaint();
  });
});
```

**Key points**

- `map.on('error')` fires for *any* MapLibre error, so you must filter for the sprite‑lookup message.
- After `addImage`, the layer that was waiting for the icon will automatically render on the next frame. No need to remove/re‑add the layer.

---

## 5. Adding **SVG** or **canvas‑generated** icons

MapLibre can accept any of the following as the second argument to `addImage`:

| Type | How to create |
|------|---------------|
| `HTMLImageElement` (PNG/JPEG/SVG) | `new Image(); img.src = url; img.onload = …` |
| `ImageBitmap` | `createImageBitmap(blob)` – useful for off‑thread decoding |
| `HTMLCanvasElement` | Draw programmatically (`ctx.fillRect`, `ctx.arc`, …) |
| `ImageData` | Raw pixel array (rarely needed) |

### Example: SVG → ImageBitmap

```js
async function addSvgIcon(name, svgString) {
  // Convert the SVG string to a Blob, then to an ImageBitmap
  const blob = new Blob([svgString], { type: 'image/svg+xml' });
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(blob);
  map.addImage(name, bitmap, { sdf: true }); // `sdf:true` lets you tint the icon via `icon-color`
}

// Usage
const mySvg = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 64 64">
                 <circle cx="32" cy="32" r="30" fill="#ff6600"/>
               </svg>`;
addSvgIcon('orange-circle', mySvg);
```

Now you can reference `'orange-circle'` in any `symbol` layer.

---

## 6. When to use **SDF** (Signed Distance Field) icons

If you want to **re‑color** an icon at runtime (e.g. change `icon-color` based on a property), you must add the image as an **SDF** image:

```js
map.addImage('my-sdf-icon', image, { sdf: true });
```

- The source image should be **monochrome** (white on transparent background).  
- After registration you can style it with `icon-color`, `icon-color-transition`, etc.

---

## 7. Updating an existing image (e.g. animated icons)

If you need to replace an image that’s already been added (e.g. a sprite that changes over time), use `map.updateImage`:

```js
map.updateImage('my-dynamic-icon', newImageElement);
map.triggerRepaint(); // optional – forces immediate redraw
```

`updateImage` keeps the same name, so any layers that already reference it will instantly show the new bitmap.

