Below is a **complete, step‑by‑step recipe** for “on‑the‑fly” icons in MapLibre GL JS when the style’s sprite does not contain the image you need.  
It works for a single icon, for many icons that you discover at runtime, and even for SVG/Canvas‑generated symbols.

---

## 1. The core idea

MapLibre can only draw an icon that is **registered in the map’s image registry** (`map.addImage`).  
If the name you put in `icon-image` is not already in the sprite, the library throws the familiar error:

```
Error: Image "my‑dynamic‑icon" does not exist in the style sprite.
```

The fix is to **load the image yourself (or create it) and call `map.addImage` before the layer tries to use it**.  
Everything else (layer definition, data‑driven `icon-image`, etc.) stays exactly the same.

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Dynamic icons</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 2️⃣ Wait for the style to be ready
  map.on('load', () => {
    // 3️⃣ Add a source with a feature that references a *dynamic* icon name
    map.addSource('places', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [{
          type: 'Feature',
          properties: { icon: 'my‑dynamic‑icon' }, // <-- name we will add later
          geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.447303, 37.753574] }
        }]
      }
    });

    // 4️⃣ Add a layer that uses the property as the icon name
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'places',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'places',
      layout: {
        'icon-image': ['get', 'icon'],   // data‑driven icon name
        'icon-size': 1,
        'icon-allow-overlap': true
      }
    });

    // 5️⃣ Load the image *after* the layer exists (any time you like)
    const iconUrl = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mapbox/maki/main/icons/restaurant-15.svg';

    // You can use map.loadImage (works for PNG, JPG, SVG‑as‑image‑element)
    map.loadImage(iconUrl, (err, image) => {
      if (err) {
        console.error('❌ Could not load icon', err);
        return;
      }
      // 6️⃣ Register the image under the exact name used in the feature
      map.addImage('my‑dynamic‑icon', image, { sdf: false });
      // The layer will instantly start drawing the icon.
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What’s happening?**

| Step | What you do | Why it matters |
|------|-------------|----------------|
| 1️⃣   | Create the map | Loads the base style (which may have *no* `my‑dynamic‑icon`). |
| 2️⃣   | `map.on('load')` | Guarantees the style’s sprite is ready; you can now add sources/layers. |
| 3️⃣   | Add a GeoJSON source with a property `icon` | This property will be read by the `icon-image` expression. |
| 4️⃣   | Add a `symbol` layer that uses `['get','icon']` | The layer will look for an image named exactly what the feature says. |
| 5️⃣   | Call `map.loadImage` (or any custom loader) | Pulls the PNG/SVG/Canvas image from wherever you want. |
| 6️⃣   | `map.addImage('my‑dynamic‑icon', image)` | Registers the image under the name the layer expects. The map instantly re‑renders with the new icon. |

---

## 3. Loading images **anywhere** in your code

You are not forced to load the image right after the `load` event.  
You can call `addImage` **any time** (even after the user has panned/zoomed or after a style change).  
Just make sure the image name you register matches the value that the layer will read.

### 3.1 Using `fetch` + `createImageBitmap` (works for PNG/JPG)

```js
async function addIconFromUrl(name, url, options = {}) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  const blob = await response.blob();
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(blob);
  map.addImage(name, bitmap, options);
}

// Example
addIconFromUrl('bike‑icon', 'https://example.com/bike.png', { sdf: false });
```

### 3.2 Using an **SVG string** (no network request)

```js
function addSvgIcon(name, svgString, options = {}) {
  const img = new Image();
  const svg = new Blob([svgString], {type: 'image/svg+xml'});
  img.src = URL.createObjectURL(svg);
  img.onload = () => {
    map.addImage(name, img, options);
    URL.revokeObjectURL(img.src);
  };
}

// Simple red circle SVG
const redCircle = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="30" height="30">
  <circle cx="15" cy="15" r="12" fill="red"/>
</svg>`;

addSvgIcon('red‑circle', redCircle, { sdf: false });
```

### 3.3 Using a **Canvas** (perfect for programmatic symbols)

```js
function addCanvasIcon(name, drawFn, width = 32, height = 32, options = {}) {
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = width;
  canvas.height = height;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  drawFn(ctx, width, height);   // user‑supplied drawing routine
  map.addImage(name, canvas, options);
}

// Example: a star
addCanvasIcon('star', (ctx, w, h) => {
  ctx.fillStyle = '#ff0';
  ctx.beginPath();
  const cx = w/2, cy = h/2, r = w/2;
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    const a = (Math.PI * 2 * i) / 5 - Math.PI/2;
    ctx.lineTo(cx + r * Math.cos(a), cy + r * Math.sin(a));
    const a2 = a + Math.PI/5;
    ctx.lineTo(cx + r/2 * Math.cos(a2), cy + r/2 * Math.sin(a2));
  }
  ctx.closePath();
  ctx.fill();
});
```

