### TL;DR  
The “official” way to deal with **icon‑image names that aren’t in your sprite** is to let MapLibre tell you when it needs an image (the `styleimagemissing` event), load or generate that image on‑the‑fly (via `fetch`, a Canvas draw, an SVG → PNG conversion, etc.), and then register it with `map.addImage()`.  
If you prefer a pure‑style solution, you can also use the **`image` expression** (`["image", url]`) which does the same thing under the hood.

Below is a step‑by‑step guide, plus a few “gotchas” and best‑practice tips that have emerged in the MapLibre community over the last couple of years.

---

## 1️⃣ What actually happens when a style references a missing sprite entry?

When the style is parsed MapLibre builds an internal **image cache** from the sprite sheet.  
If a layer’s `icon-image` (or any other property that expects an image) refers to a name that isn’t in that cache, MapLibre:

1. **Emits a `styleimagemissing` event** on the `Map` instance.  
2. **Falls back to the “missing‑image” placeholder** (a transparent 1 × 1 PNG) so the map can keep rendering while you fetch the real thing.

That event is the hook you want to use.

> **Why not just pre‑populate the sprite?**  
> Sprites are great for static assets, but they bloat the initial payload and make it impossible to generate icons that depend on user data (e.g., a colour‑coded badge, a QR code, a chart thumbnail, etc.). The on‑demand flow lets you keep the sprite tiny and still have an infinite namespace of icons.

---

## 2️⃣ The idiomatic “listen‑and‑add” pattern

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (any version ≥ 2.0.0)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// 2️⃣ Hook into the missing‑image event
map.on('styleimagemissing', async (e) => {
  const missingName = e.id;               // the name the style asked for
  console.log(`Missing image: ${missingName}`);

  // 3️⃣ Decide *how* you will obtain it.
  //    a) From a remote PNG/SVG URL:
  //    b) From a Canvas/SVG you generate on the fly.
  //    c) From a data‑URI you already have in memory.
  //    Choose one of the helpers below.

  try {
    const image = await loadOrGenerateImage(missingName);
    // 4️⃣ Register it with the map (the third argument is optional)
    map.addImage(missingName, image, { sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2 });
    // 5️⃣ Force a repaint of the layers that were waiting on this image.
    //    (Usually not needed – MapLibre will automatically re‑draw once the
    //    image is in the cache – but it can be useful if you’re in a tight
    //    animation loop.)
    map.triggerRepaint();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Could not load image "${missingName}":`, err);
    // Optionally add a fallback image so the UI doesn’t stay blank.
    map.addImage(missingName, fallbackImage);
  }
});
```

### 2.1 Helper: Load a remote PNG/SVG

```js
async function loadOrGenerateImage(name) {
  // Convention: the name is a URL‑safe slug that maps to a real endpoint.
  // e.g. "badge‑red‑42" → "https://my.cdn.com/icons/badge-red-42.svg"
  const url = `https://my.cdn.com/icons/${name}.svg`;

  // MapLibre can load PNGs directly via `map.loadImage`, but for SVGs we
  // need to rasterise them first (Canvas → ImageBitmap).
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);

  const blob = await response.blob();

  // If the blob is already a raster format (PNG/JPEG) we can skip the canvas.
  if (blob.type.startsWith('image/png') || blob.type.startsWith('image/jpeg')) {
    return await createImageBitmap(blob);
  }

  // For SVG we rasterise onto a canvas (preserves vector crispness at any DPI).
  const svgText = await blob.text();
  return rasteriseSvg(svgText, 64, 64); // 64 px square, adjust as needed
}
```

### 2.2 Helper: Rasterise an SVG string onto a Canvas

```js
function rasteriseSvg(svgString, width, height) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important: give the browser a data‑URI so it can decode the SVG.
    img.src = 'data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,' + encodeURIComponent(svgString);
    img.onload = () => {
      const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
      canvas.width = width;
      canvas.height = height;
      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
      // Optional: set a background colour if you need an opaque icon.
      // ctx.fillStyle = 'white';
      // ctx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);
      ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0, width, height);
      canvas.toBlob((blob) => {
        if (!blob) return reject(new Error('Canvas toBlob failed'));
        createImageBitmap(blob).then(resolve, reject);
      });
    };
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(e);
  });
}
```

### 2.3 Helper: Generate a Canvas‑based badge on the fly

```js
function generateBadge(name) {
  // Example: name = "badge‑blue‑7"
  const [_, colour, number] = name.split('-'); // ["badge","blue","7"]
  const size = 48;
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

  // Circle background
  ctx.fillStyle = colour;
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();

  // Number label
  ctx.fillStyle = 'white';
  ctx.font = 'bold 24px sans-serif';
  ctx.textAlign = 'center';
  ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
  ctx.fillText(number, size / 2, size / 2 + 1); // +1 for optical centering

  return createImageBitmap(canvas);
}
```

You can combine any of the above strategies inside `loadOrGenerateImage()` – the only contract is that it **returns a `HTMLImageElement`, `HTMLCanvasElement`, `ImageBitmap`, or `ImageData`** that MapLibre can ingest.

